# XCU cd
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# cd is intrinsic (XCU 1.7). The test makes its own tree of directories,
# marked by empty files, and tells where it is by those marks and by the
# PWD variable, never by a command that is not under test. Symbolic links
# come from ln -s, and every test that needs one reports UNRESOLVED when
# it cannot be made.

assertion  XCU.cd.001
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/cd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/cd.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/cd.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       cd with a relative or an absolute directory operand changes
           the working directory of the shell, exits zero and, when no
           CDPATH directory and no hyphen-minus is used, writes nothing
           to standard output.
test       t/001-change.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.002
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/cd.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
class      required
since      1992
says       After a successful cd the PWD variable names the new directory
           as an absolute pathname, and a dot-dot component in the
           operand is resolved by removing the component before it
           (for a directory that exists).
test       t/002-pwd.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.003
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/cd.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
class      required
since      1992
says       After a successful cd the OLDPWD variable holds the directory
           the shell was in before the call.
test       t/003-oldpwd.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.004
anchor     utilities/cd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/cd.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       cd - changes to the directory in OLDPWD and writes the new
           directory (the value of PWD) followed by a newline to standard
           output.
test       t/004-hyphen.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.005
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/cd.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
class      required
since      1992
says       cd with no operand goes to the directory in HOME when HOME is
           set to a non-empty value.
test       t/005-home.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.006
anchor     utilities/cd.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       By default, and with -L, a symbolic link in the operand is kept
           in PWD and a following dot-dot goes back to the directory
           holding the link, not to the parent of the link target.
test       t/006-logical.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.007
anchor     utilities/cd.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With -P symbolic links are resolved: PWD holds the physical
           pathname without the link, and a dot-dot goes to the parent
           of the link target.
test       t/007-physical.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.008
anchor     utilities/cd.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       When -L and -P are both given the last one decides.
test       t/008-last-option.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.009
anchor     utilities/cd.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/cd.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       A relative operand not starting with dot or dot-dot is looked up
           in the directories of CDPATH in order; when one is found the
           shell goes there and the new directory is written to standard
           output.
test       t/009-cdpath.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.010
anchor     utilities/cd.html#ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/cd.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       An empty CDPATH entry stands for the current directory and is
           tried in its position in the list; when that entry is the one
           used nothing is written, and an operand starting with ./ or
           ../ is not looked up in CDPATH at all.
test       t/010-cdpath-order.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.011
anchor     utilities/cd.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
anchor     utilities/cd.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4
class      required
since      1992
says       cd to a directory that does not exist or to a file that is not a
           directory exits greater than zero with a diagnostic on
           standard error, and the working directory and PWD stay as
           they were.
test       t/011-error.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.012
anchor     utilities/cd.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/cd.html#CONSEQUENCES OF ERRORS
class      required
since      1992
says       An empty directory operand makes cd write a diagnostic and exit
           greater than zero without changing the directory.
test       t/012-empty-operand.sh

assertion  XCU.cd.013
anchor     utilities/cd.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       In a logical cd a dot-dot component whose preceding component is
           not a directory (missing, or a regular file) is an error: cd
           exits greater than zero and does not change the directory.
test       t/013-dotdot-error.sh
